    "content": "Somalia's Finance Minister Bihi Imaan Egeh spoke in Mogadishu on Tuesday, alerting the public to a significant drop in national revenue. He said the country is missing between $450 million and $600 million that previously supported the economy, stressing the need to accelerate private sector development as a way to compensate for the loss.\n\nThe warning comes at a time when Somalia's federal budget is under increasing strain. A policy paper published in July 2026 projected a $29 million fiscal deficit for the current year and highlighted the government's continued heavy reliance on foreign grants to fund its operations.\n\nEgeh characterized the push for private enterprise not as an optional policy choice but as an urgent necessity. He pointed to shrinking external support as a key factor driving the need for domestic economic self-sufficiency, urging businesses and investors to step up their role in filling the revenue gap.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development underscores the importance of remittances and business investments in Somalia's economic stability. Many diaspora members already contribute significantly to the economy through transfers and ventures, but the minister's remarks suggest that more robust private sector activity is essential to offset declining state revenue and foreign aid.\n\nThe government has not yet announced specific measures to boost private sector growth, but Egeh's comments signal that fiscal challenges may prompt new policies to stimulate local enterprise and reduce dependency on external funding.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/07/29/finance-minister-sounds-alarm-over-vanishing-revenue/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=finance-minister-sounds-alarm-over-vanishing-revenue",
